<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext is permitted because the ingest relay is plain HTTP on the local network.

  The launcher POSTs to http://<syncHost>:8787/api/sync to start a library sync. Without this,
  Android 9+ blocks the call outright and the ingest tile reports
  "Failed to trigger host daemon: Cleartext HTTP traffic to <host> not permitted", which is
  exactly what it was doing: the file existed in this module's res/xml but the manifest never
  referenced it, so it had no effect at all.

  This is deliberately a blanket permit rather than a domain allowlist, and that is a real
  tradeoff worth stating. Android's network security config matches on hostnames and cannot
  express "any RFC1918 address", and the sync host is entered by the user at runtime, so the one
  host that actually needs cleartext is precisely the one that cannot be enumerated here.

  What this does NOT do is downgrade anything that is already HTTPS. Open-Meteo, BeaconDB, Apple
  WPS and Wikidata are all requested over https:// in code and stay encrypted; permitting
  cleartext only means a plain-http URL is allowed to connect, not that an https one is weakened.
  The trust anchors below keep the normal system CAs and additionally honour user-installed ones,
  which is what lets a self-hosted relay be put behind a private CA later if that is ever wanted.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
